Study 1: "Frozen shoulder: prospective clinical study with an evaluation of three treatment regimens"                   
Abstract: Forty-two patients with frozen shoulder were followed up closely for eight months. They were all taught       
pendular exercises and randomly allocated to one of four treatment groups: (a) intraarticular steroids, (b)             
mobilisations , (c) ice therapy, (d) no treatment. This study has shown that there is little long-term advantage in any 
of the treatment regimens but that steroid injections may benefit pain and range of movement in the early stages of the 
condition.                                                                                                              
                                                                                                                        
- Design: prospective clinical study with random allocation to four groups. Sounds like RCT (randomized). Yes, random   
allocation to groups.                                                                                                   
- Population: patients with frozen shoulder (adhesive capsulitis). Adults? Likely adults.                               
- Intervention: mobilisations (manual therapy). Also intraarticular steroids (not manual/exercise), ice therapy (not    
manual/exercise), no treatment.                                                                                         
- Comparison: comparing mobilisations vs steroids vs ice vs no treatment. So includes comparison of manual therapy      
(mobilisations) vs other interventions (steroids, ice, no treatment). This fits: comparing manual therapy vs placebo/no 
intervention/different type/manual therapy/other intervention.                                                          
- Outcomes: not explicitly listed but they evaluated pain and range of movement. Likely includes pain and ROM.          
Thus appears to meet criteria: RCT, adults with adhesive capsulitis, manual therapy (mobilisations) vs other            
interventions. Should INCLUDE.                                                                                          
                                                                                                                        
